1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,215
A cricket ball thrown up.

2
00:00:02,215 --> 00:00:04,087
A car braking at a signal.

3
00:00:04,087 --> 00:00:06,205
A bicycle rolling down a slope.

4
00:00:06,605 --> 00:00:08,466
Three very different moments.

5
00:00:08,466 --> 00:00:11,977
But one set of three equations
can predict all of them.

6
00:00:12,428 --> 00:00:15,321
Aarav starts from rest
at the top of a slope.

7
00:00:15,321 --> 00:00:18,672
His bicycle gains two metres
per second, every second.

8
00:00:19,022 --> 00:00:22,608
Without riding it, can he know
his speed after five seconds?

9
00:00:22,608 --> 00:00:24,459
And how far down he will be?

10
00:00:24,959 --> 00:00:25,859
Yes.

11
00:00:25,486 --> 00:00:30,189
If the acceleration stays constant,
three equations tell him everything.

12
00:00:30,189 --> 00:00:31,460
Let's build them.

13
00:00:32,110 --> 00:00:33,499
First, what do we need?

14
00:00:33,849 --> 00:00:35,244
Five quantities.

15
00:00:35,244 --> 00:00:37,556
Initial velocity, v nought.

16
00:00:37,556 --> 00:00:39,260
Final velocity, v.

17
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:40,774
Acceleration, a.

18
00:00:40,774 --> 00:00:41,811
Time, t.

19
00:00:41,811 --> 00:00:43,635
And displacement, x.

20
00:00:44,035 --> 00:00:47,431
The one condition: the
acceleration must be uniform.

21
00:00:47,431 --> 00:00:50,273
Same size, same direction, all the time.

22
00:00:50,873 --> 00:00:53,702
Let's run Aarav's ride
on our motion bench.

23
00:00:54,052 --> 00:00:55,470
He starts at zero.

24
00:00:55,470 --> 00:00:58,766
The acceleration is two
metres per second squared.

25
00:00:59,166 --> 00:01:00,839
Watch the velocity arrow.

26
00:01:00,839 --> 00:01:04,551
Every second, it grows by the
same two metres per second.

27
00:01:04,951 --> 00:01:06,900
And the paint marks spread out.

28
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:10,131
One metre, three metres,
five metres, seven metres.

29
00:01:10,631 --> 00:01:13,684
Let's replay the first two
seconds in slow motion.

30
00:01:14,084 --> 00:01:15,200
Now the graph.

31
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:18,697
Velocity against time is a
straight line, sloping up.

32
00:01:18,697 --> 00:01:20,722
Its slope is the acceleration.

33
00:01:21,372 --> 00:01:24,160
Equation one, from the
meaning of acceleration.

34
00:01:24,510 --> 00:01:28,585
Acceleration is the change
in velocity divided by time.

35
00:01:28,585 --> 00:01:31,739
So a equals v minus v nought, over t.

36
00:01:32,489 --> 00:01:36,154
Multiply both sides by t,
and move v nought across.

37
00:01:36,154 --> 00:01:38,268
v equals v nought plus a t.

38
00:01:39,018 --> 00:01:43,953
In words: final velocity is the
starting velocity, plus the velocity you

39
00:01:43,953 --> 00:01:44,853
gain.

40
00:01:45,258 --> 00:01:48,007
Equation two, from the
area under the graph.

41
00:01:48,357 --> 00:01:51,973
The area under a velocity time
graph is the displacement.

42
00:01:51,973 --> 00:01:54,036
Here it splits into two parts.

43
00:01:54,786 --> 00:01:56,942
A rectangle, v nought times t.

44
00:01:56,942 --> 00:02:00,209
That is how far you would
go with no acceleration.

45
00:02:00,959 --> 00:02:03,611
And a triangle, half of t times a t.

46
00:02:03,611 --> 00:02:06,962
That is the extra distance
you gain by speeding up.

47
00:02:07,712 --> 00:02:08,612
Add them.

48
00:02:08,456 --> 00:02:11,270
x equals v nought t,
plus half a t squared.

49
00:02:11,970 --> 00:02:14,596
Equation three, when time is not given.

50
00:02:14,946 --> 00:02:18,906
From equation one, t equals
v minus v nought, over a.

51
00:02:18,906 --> 00:02:21,718
Put this into equation two and simplify.

52
00:02:22,468 --> 00:02:23,583
Time disappears.

53
00:02:23,583 --> 00:02:26,775
v squared equals v nought
squared, plus two a x.

54
00:02:27,475 --> 00:02:28,562
Remember this.

55
00:02:28,562 --> 00:02:30,735
Three equations, one condition.

56
00:02:30,735 --> 00:02:33,123
The acceleration must stay constant.

57
00:02:33,823 --> 00:02:34,925
Side by side.

58
00:02:34,925 --> 00:02:36,864
Which equation do you pick?

59
00:02:37,214 --> 00:02:40,170
Each equation leaves out one quantity.

60
00:02:40,170 --> 00:02:42,113
Equation one has no x.

61
00:02:42,113 --> 00:02:44,057
Equation two has no v.

62
00:02:44,057 --> 00:02:46,118
Equation three has no t.

63
00:02:46,568 --> 00:02:48,166
So look at the question.

64
00:02:48,166 --> 00:02:51,081
Find the quantity it
neither gives nor asks for.

65
00:02:51,081 --> 00:02:52,908
Pick the equation without it.

66
00:02:53,508 --> 00:02:54,579
Now you try.

67
00:02:54,579 --> 00:02:59,553
A car moving at twenty metres per second
brakes with a constant deceleration,

68
00:02:59,553 --> 00:03:01,478
and stops in forty metres.

69
00:03:01,478 --> 00:03:04,349
To find the deceleration, which equation?

70
00:03:04,349 --> 00:03:05,681
Pause and decide.

71
00:03:08,881 --> 00:03:10,029
Equation three!

72
00:03:10,029 --> 00:03:14,149
Time is neither given nor asked,
so pick the one without t.

73
00:03:14,149 --> 00:03:18,803
Zero equals four hundred minus eighty
a, so a is five metres per second

74
00:03:18,803 --> 00:03:20,843
squared, against the motion.

75
00:03:21,493 --> 00:03:23,150
Now, Aarav's question.

76
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:25,784
Speed and distance after five seconds.

77
00:03:26,134 --> 00:03:27,391
v nought is zero.

78
00:03:27,391 --> 00:03:29,662
a is two metres per second squared.

79
00:03:29,662 --> 00:03:30,966
t is five seconds.

80
00:03:31,466 --> 00:03:32,478
Equation one.

81
00:03:32,478 --> 00:03:34,830
v equals zero plus two times five.

82
00:03:34,830 --> 00:03:36,923
That is ten metres per second.

83
00:03:37,673 --> 00:03:38,715
Equation two.

84
00:03:38,715 --> 00:03:42,238
x equals zero plus half
times two times twenty five.

85
00:03:42,238 --> 00:03:44,178
That is twenty five metres.

86
00:03:44,928 --> 00:03:46,745
Check with equation three.

87
00:03:46,745 --> 00:03:48,673
Ten squared is one hundred.

88
00:03:48,673 --> 00:03:52,175
Two times two times twenty
five is also one hundred.

89
00:03:52,175 --> 00:03:53,108
It matches.

90
00:03:53,758 --> 00:03:55,221
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:03:55,571 --> 00:04:00,384
Many students use these equations for
any motion, even when the acceleration

92
00:04:00,384 --> 00:04:01,284
changes.

93
00:04:01,466 --> 00:04:04,952
But they work only when the
acceleration is constant.

94
00:04:04,952 --> 00:04:08,074
If a changes with time,
use calculus instead.

95
00:04:08,074 --> 00:04:11,358
And keep signs: a
deceleration is a negative a.

96
00:04:12,008 --> 00:04:13,500
Where is this asked?

97
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:18,517
As numericals: stopping distance, braking,
and finding time, speed or distance

98
00:04:18,517 --> 00:04:20,300
with constant acceleration.

99
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:22,137
Exam level now.

100
00:04:22,137 --> 00:04:26,878
A body starts from rest with constant
acceleration and covers twelve metres

101
00:04:26,878 --> 00:04:28,440
in the third second.

102
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,045
Find its acceleration.

103
00:04:30,045 --> 00:04:31,335
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:36,735 --> 00:04:37,987
The answer is C.

105
00:04:37,987 --> 00:04:40,723
Four point eight metres
per second squared.

106
00:04:41,223 --> 00:04:45,591
Distance in the third second is the
distance in three seconds, minus the

107
00:04:45,591 --> 00:04:47,220
distance in two seconds.

108
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:51,123
That is half a times nine,
minus half a times four.

109
00:04:51,123 --> 00:04:53,236
Half a times five equals twelve.

110
00:04:53,236 --> 00:04:56,439
So a is twenty four over
five, four point eight.

111
00:04:57,039 --> 00:04:58,500
Here is your recap.

112
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:00,001
Screenshot this card.

113
00:05:03,201 --> 00:05:06,038
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

114
00:05:06,438 --> 00:05:07,338
One.

115
00:05:06,962 --> 00:05:11,566
Which quantity is missing from v
squared equals v nought squared plus two

116
00:05:11,566 --> 00:05:12,466
a x?

117
00:05:13,051 --> 00:05:13,951
Two.

118
00:05:13,536 --> 00:05:18,142
Where does the half a t squared term
come from on the velocity time graph?

119
00:05:19,042 --> 00:05:19,942
Three.

120
00:05:19,633 --> 00:05:22,425
When can you not use
these three equations?

121
00:05:23,525 --> 00:05:27,633
Next time: drop a stone and a
feather where there is no air.

122
00:05:27,633 --> 00:05:28,956
Which lands first?

123
00:05:28,956 --> 00:05:30,428
Motion under gravity.
